{
 "thread": "plaNeAMjdvM",
 "subject": "Discretized model instead of area search model?",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2024-01-30T15:25:05Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "eNZVuHQ9AAAJ",
   "from": "Marina Ramon",
   "author": "d223449404b731ae",
   "date": "2024-01-29T10:34:33Z",
   "subject": "Discretized model instead of area search model?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:black;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\">Hi all,</span><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:black;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\">I am Marina Ramon. I\r\nam trying to estimate chimpanzee density using genetic data from faecal\r\nsamples. I am using CL models for area searches. The area searched has a\r\ncomplex shape (i.e. ~ 50 polygons grouping searched cells) and this makes the\r\nmodel fitting very slow.</span><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:black;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\">As an alternative, I\r\ntried a CL model for count detectors after discretization, which allowed me to\r\nfit the model much more quickly. The complex shape of the area searched already\r\nrequired me to define the search area as a subset of cells within a regular\r\ngrid, so it was straightforward to treat the centroid of each searched cell as\r\na detector. Estimates from one successfully-fitted area search model matched\r\nthose from the discretized model (the density estimates were practically\r\nidentical). </span><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:black;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\">So far, I’ve tried\r\nwith null models, but now I would like to incorporate covariates and also try to fit an h2 model. I was\r\nthinking of using a CL model for count detectors instead of a CL model for area\r\nsearches to make sure I can fit the models. How appropriate would be to treat\r\ndata from area searches as if they arose from an array of detectors? </span><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:black;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\">Thank you very much in\r\nadvance.</span><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:black;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\">Marina</span><span lang=\"EN-CA\" style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;border:1pt none windowtext;padding:0cm\"></span></p>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "wFNOQ0gHAAAJ",
   "from": "Murray Efford",
   "author": "118cea91a1619106",
   "date": "2024-01-30T05:07:05Z",
   "subject": "Re: Discretized model instead of area search model?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>Hi</div><div>As you reported, the two approaches (polygon search, polygon discretized as point many detectors) can give very similar estimates. I would expect this to hold even for covariate models. There will be some differences in the covariate effects themselves if you are talking about detector-level covariates, but that seems unlikely. Occasion and individual covariates of detection parameters should be fine. Also spatial covariates of density if you leave CL behind.</div><div>Murray<br></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
